WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.250
So, there’s this fantastic Wikipedia list
called Wikipedia:Unusual Articles.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:09.519
It’s filled with unusual, unexpected, and
unbelievable subjects.

00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:13.419
Every other week I click on a new link, and
teach you a little bit more about our amazing

00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:14.540
world.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.870
Do you want to live forever?

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:20.900
Well, if Wolverine or Deadpool taught you
anything, you don’t, but there is a theory

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:21.900
on how you can.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.632
It’s gonna need a bit of explanation so
if you’re like this guy and don’t enjoy

00:00:25.632 --> 00:00:29.509
long-winded answers, I suggest you turn off
the video now.

00:00:29.509 --> 00:00:30.509
Quantum Mechanics.

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:31.509
They’re complicated.

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:35.739
What we’re going to talk about today is
only a theory because it’s practically impossible

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:36.739
to test.

00:00:36.739 --> 00:00:40.239
One of the bases of quantum mechanics is Heisenberg’s
uncertainty principle.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:45.289
Heisenberg says that, in quantum mechanics,
“the more precisely the position is determined,

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:48.960
the less precisely the momentum is known in
this instant, and vice versa.”

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.420
Let me explain.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:51.930
Imagine you’re a blind man.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.690
In order to tell how far away an object is,
you’ve developed a system in which you throw

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:59.130
a tennis ball at the object, let say it’s
a larger medicine ball.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.280
If your tennis ball bounces back in a short
amount of time, you know that the medicine

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.160
ball is close, if it bounces back in a longer
amount of time, the medicine ball is farther.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.320
The problem is, however, that through observing
where the medicine ball is, you’ve moved

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:15.230
it and it’s therefore no longer in the position
in which you observed it.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:19.710
In the subatomic world, it’s essentially
impossible to observe an object without affecting

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.220
it, so all quantum theories must be based
on and proven by thought experiments.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.990
Alright, that was my best attempt at summarizing
a concept that you can study for years in

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:30.270
30 seconds.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.720
If you want to learn more, you can click here
for a Veritasium video about the subject.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.340
The first theory in our long-winded explanation
is the Many-Worlds theory.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.430
Let’s say you’re standing on top of a
cliff.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:43.390
You’re making the decision on whether or
not to jump.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:47.320
According to the many-worlds theory, at the
very moment that your decision is made, the

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.540
universe splits into two—one copy being
the timeline in which you jump off the cliff,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.250
the other being the one where you walk away.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.910
Of course, the you that jumped off the cliff
doesn’t know that the universe split into

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:01.970
two and in your reality, life just continues
on as normal while simultaneously in a different

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:06.460
universe, a copy of you continues his life
following his decision to not jump from the

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.460
cliff.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:12.590
The many worlds theory says that every single
time a decision is made by anyone or anything,

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:18.350
the universe splits and and your self inhabits
only one of nearly infinite different universes.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:19.910
Now to quantum suicide.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.800
In this thought experiment, you, the experimenter,
are standing in a box with a nuclear bomb

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.950
that, if exploded, will immediately and unconditionally
kill you.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:34.690
Hooked up to the bomb is a trigger that measures
the spin value of a proton every three seconds.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:39.890
If the proton spins one way, there’s just
a click sound, the other way, the bomb explodes.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:44.810
Statistically, the proton should have a 50%
of spinning one way or another so the chance

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:47.440
of the bomb going off or not is truly random.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:56.140
Three, two, one, (click), three, two, one,
(click), three, two, one, (click).

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:01.640
At this point, there’s only a 12.5% that
the bomb not going off was random, we’ll

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:08.280
keep going, three, two, one, (click), three,
two, one, (click).

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.160
If we fast forward 300 seconds, after the
proton has been measured 100 times, there’s

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.850
only a eight hundred-octillionth of a percent
(0.00000000000000000000000000008%) chance

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.140
that your survival is random.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.540
We can now conclude beyond a reasonable doubt
that you are immortal.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.760
What happened is that every time the proton
was measured, every time the bomb could have

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.630
gone off, the universe split into two, one
being the scenario in which you die, the other

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.420
being the one in which you live.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.940
But why didn’t you go to the universe where
the bomb went off, why didn’t you go to

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:37.940
the one where you died?

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.660
Well it’s quite simple.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.410
The version of you who died cannot know that
he died, only the version of you who lived

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:46.170
can know that you lived.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.480
The next time the bomb went off, the same
situation occurred.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.780
Only the living version of you continues on
in consciousness.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.410
On and on this will continue, forever, because
the version of you who lives can continue

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.060
to realize he is living.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.450
You can’t die because only your living self
will keep consciousness and existence.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.430
This, of course, brings up a lot of existential
questions.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:10.150
Will the version of yourself who lives be
the same person?

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.030
Are the people around you in the new universe
the same people?

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.930
Shouldn’t everyone be immortal from any
sort of death in some universe?

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.510
What does it mean to be a person if there
are so many versions of you?

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.100
There are certainly flaws to this theory and
there are certainly unanswerable questions.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.850
Should you go off and try quantum suicide
in order to prove your own immortality?

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.220
Well, I’d suggest not.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.050
This whole theory is based on the many-worlds
interpretation of quantum mechanics which

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:38.190
is only the second or third most popular interpretation.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:42.160
The Copenhagen theory, which you know from
Schrödinger's cat, is the leading interpretation

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.010
of quantum mechanics and would lead to you
being properly, and unconditionally dead.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.420
Thank you for watching.

